# pkgctl: init's approvals of a package's programs (docs/M13.7-PLAN.md
# section 9, track PO5), on the channel init makes for it
# (initctl.pkg_channel), which the shell hands to bin/pkg when the owner
# types `pkg`. Served by init (user/services/init/pkg.c, the checks in
# pkgapprove.c); called by bin/pkg and utest.
#
# A program on /data runs only with an approval line in /data/etc/allow
# (<dataprog.h>), which only init and the shell can write. A package's
# lines are init's: bin/pkg writes the files under /data/pkg (its view
# alone may: FS_VIEW_GUARD_PKG, <fsview.h>), then asks init to approve
# them, sending the package's signed manifest. init trusts nothing bin/pkg
# says: it checks the manifest's signature with /boot/ports.pub (a build
# without one approves nothing), reads every program back from /data/pkg
# itself into memory only it holds, and from those bytes checks the file's
# size and SHA-256 against the manifest's `file` line, makes the page
# table and checks its SHA-256 against the `table` line, checks that the
# program's own list (its ELF note) is the manifest's `program` list, and
# applies the refusals `allow` applies (dp_wants_refused). Only when every
# program has passed does it write the tables (/data/etc/allow.d/<sha-256>)
# and then the lines, each tagged with the package:
#
#     <sha-256>\t/data/pkg/<path>\t<what its list asks for>\tpkg=<name> <version>-r<revision>
#
# The shell's `allow -l` shows the tag; its `allow -r` and `allow <file>`
# refuse to drop a tagged line: revoke does, for `pkg remove` and an
# upgrade's old version.
#
# Both methods are answered later, from init's loop, once its worker thread
# has done the work (a program of 64 MiB takes a moment to read and hash):
# one at a time per channel, the next read when the last is answered. One
# channel at a time: a newer pkg_channel closes an older one.
#
# Restart: none. After ERR_PEER_CLOSED init is gone; nothing was written
# that an answer didn't report (a line is written whole with the others, or
# not at all), and the next `pkg` settles the journal.

protocol pkgctl 85

# The longest name and version (the manifest grammar's), and the longest
# reason.
const NAME_MAX 63
const WHY_MAX  95

# approve's flags: the programs are staged, at `<path>.new` (an install's or
# an upgrade's files before the swap); the lines name `<path>` all the same.
const STAGED 1

# Approve the programs of the package whose manifest (the signed text,
# manifest_bytes of the VMO, at most 4 MiB: PKG_MANIFEST_MAX) is given.
# The answer: verdict OK and the number of programs approved (a package
# without programs approves nothing and is OK), or the refusal: verdict
# ERR_ACCESS_DENIED (no ports key, not the key's signature, a file or a
# table that isn't the manifest's, a list that isn't the program's own or
# that asks for what no program from /data may have, a path another
# package's line holds), ERR_INVALID_ARGS (the manifest's grammar),
# ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED (a must-understand line this build doesn't know),
# ERR_NOT_FOUND (a program isn't there), ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE (a size), or the
# filesystem's error; program: the index of the program a refusal is about
# (in the manifest's order), 0xffffffff if none; why: a reason for the
# owner. A refusal writes nothing. Approving a package again writes no
# second line for the same (hash, path, package).
1  approve  (u32 flags, u64 manifest_bytes, handle<vmo> manifest) -> (i32 verdict, u32 programs, u32 program, str[<=95] why) later

# Take back every line tagged with this package (its version and
# revision): the lines go, and the tables no line names any more. lines:
# how many went (0: none had the tag). ERR_INVALID_ARGS: not a name, a
# version or a revision the manifest grammar takes; the filesystem's
# errors (then nothing changed).
2  revoke  (str[<=63] name, str[<=63] version, u32 revision) -> (u32 lines) later
